# The Closure Scope Issue in Laravel Collections

When working with Laravel collections, it is easy to assume that variables passed into closures are always accessible via `use`. However, the behavior of the `contains` method specifically can trip up developers who expect standard closure scoping rules to apply universally. In your specific case, you are trying to determine if a product ID exists within an unavailability list, but the logic fails because of how the `contains` method handles its closure arguments.

The core issue lies in the signature and behavior of the `contains` method when used as a callback. While standard PHP closures allow you to capture variables from the outer scope using `use`, Laravel's `Collection::contains` (and its alias `whereIn`) expects the closure to receive specific arguments: `$value` and `$key`. These represent the current item being iterated over within the collection and its corresponding key index, respectively.

In your code snippet, you are attempting to access `$product` inside the closure passed to `contains`:

```php
'unavailableProducts'->contains(function ($value, $key) use ($product) {
    // ...
})
```

This syntax is technically valid PHP for defining a closure that uses an outer variable. However, there is a subtle but critical distinction in how Laravel processes the closure arguments versus the `use` variables. If the closure logic relies on `$product`, you must ensure that the scope is correctly captured. But looking closer at your specific comparison logic:

```php
if ($value->product_id == $product->id) { ... }
```

The real problem isn't necessarily the `use` clause itself, but rather how the method `contains` evaluates the truthiness of the closure. The `contains` method returns `true` if the callback returns a "truthy" value for any item. Your logic returns `false` only when an ID match is found, and `true` otherwise. Consequently, if *any* item in `$unavailableProducts` does not match the current product, `contains` will return `true`. This means your `available` flag becomes true even if a match was found earlier in the loop, simply because subsequent items don't match.

The standard expectation with `contains` is that it acts as an existential check: "Does there exist at least one item that satisfies this condition?" When you invert the logic to return `false` on a match and `true` otherwise, you are effectively asking "Is there at least one non-matching item?", which almost always returns true unless every single item matches.

To fix this, you should utilize the negation of the check or switch to a method designed for checking existence directly. The most efficient approach is to use `contains` with a predicate that explicitly checks for the presence of the product ID without inverting the logic inside the closure. Alternatively, using `every` might be more semantically aligned if you want to verify that *no* unavailable products match the current one.

Here is a corrected pattern using `every`:

```php
$allProducts = $products->map(function ($product) use ($unavailableProducts) {
    return [
        'id'            => $product->id,
        'title'         => $product->title,
        'available'     => $unavailableProducts['result']->every(function ($item) use ($product) {
            // Returns true if the current unavailable item DOES NOT match the product
            return $item->product_id != $product->id;
        }),
    ];
});
```

In this version, `every` returns `true` only if *all* items in `$unavailableProducts['result']` satisfy the condition (i.e., none of them have an ID matching `$product`). This aligns perfectly with the definition of "available." If even one item matches, `every` returns `false`, correctly marking the product as unavailable.

If you prefer to stick with `contains`, you must ensure your predicate defines a positive match and then negate the result:

```php
$allProducts = $products->map(function ($product) use ($unavailableProducts) {
    return [
        'id'            => $product->id,
        'title'         => $product->title,
        'available'     => !$unavailableProducts['result']->contains(function ($item) use ($product) {
            return $item->product_id == $product->id;
        }),
    ];
});
```

Here, `contains` finds the first match and returns `true`. The negation operator `!` flips it to `false`, indicating the product is not available. This is often more readable because it mirrors natural language: "Is there an unavailable product with this ID?" followed by "Not available."

When dealing with large datasets, be mindful of performance implications. If `$unavailableProducts['result']` is a massive collection, calling `contains` (or `every`) inside a `map` loop creates an O(nÂ²) complexity scenario if not optimized. In production scenarios involving thousands of products, consider converting the unavailability list to an associative array keyed by ID using `pluck` before the mapping loop:

```php
$unavailableIds = collect($unavailableProducts['result'])->pluck('product_id')->toArray();

$allProducts = $products->map(function ($product) use ($unavailableIds) {
    return [
        'id'            => $product->id,
        'title'         => $product->title,
        'available'     => !in_array($product->id, $unavailableIds),
    ];
});
```

This reduces the complexity to O(n) by utilizing native array lookups, ensuring your application remains responsive even as data volumes grow.
